//! The pure decision kernel of the host-side network gate.
//!
//! Functions over owned data: bytes in, value out. No I/O, no clock, no
//! locks, no logging, no dependencies. The shell reads sockets and calls in.
//!
//! # Theorems
//!
//! | Id  | Statement | Where | Tool |
//! |-----|-----------|-------|------|
//! | P1  | `parse_frame` returns for every byte string | [`frame::parse_frame`] | Verus (no panic path), Kani |
//! | P2  | Every fact `parse_frame` reports is a fact of the IPv4 datagram or ARP packet a receiver acts on: version 4, a header of 20 bytes or more, a total length that covers the header and fits the frame; a port only for TCP or UDP, only on a first fragment, only from a whole header inside the total length; a UDP length that runs past the total length leaves the ports readable only when more fragments follow, and the payload reported is then what this fragment carries | `frame::facts_sound` | Verus, Kani |
//! | P2c | A frame that carries such a datagram is never reported as [`frame::Facts::Other`] | [`frame::parse_frame`] | Verus, Kani |
//! | A1  | For every block `Plan::new` accepts: the seven reserved addresses are pairwise distinct and inside the block, the lease run is not empty, and every leasable address is inside the block and not reserved | `plan::theorem_a1` | Verus, Kani |
//! | G7  | A table never holds more than `cap` entries, more than `quota` for one row, or two entries with one row and key, and its quota never exceeds its capacity; an insert that would break a bound changes nothing and says which; what it holds after an entry is added does not depend on where the entry is put (`pool::theorem_added_members`). The record tables are [`slab::Slab`]s, which keep G7 as [`pool::Pool`] does (SB1) | [`slab::Slab::insert`], `slab::sinv`; [`pool::Pool::insert`], `pool::inv`; `pool::within_caps` | Verus; Kani: `Pool`, one operation from any table of up to four entries; `Slab`, per shape (SB1) |
//! | G6  | A box frame is admitted as a reply exactly when it names this box, is TCP or UDP, and the table holds an unexpired record for the same client, protocol and ports; a reply never opens a record, and a refused reply changes nothing but removing the frame's own record when it has expired, as does an inbound frame that opens nothing and finds no live record of its own; a TCP segment with FIN marks its record as closed by the side that sent it and one with RST as closed by both: the first FIN sets the record's life to the closing window, the second FIN or an RST to the closed window, after which nothing extends it; an opening packet from the client reopens a closing record; no frame of its conversation removes a live record; only an opening packet addressed to this box (a UDP datagram, a TCP SYN without ACK) opens one; a refresh never shortens a record or sets it past the frame's instant plus its window, and only the box's reply marks a UDP record replied or an open TCP record answered (`flow::theorem_g6h_only_the_box_answers`). The table has a capacity and no bound per client. A new conversation is refused only at the capacity, over a table that holds no record closed by both sides, and when a sweep was due it found nothing expired and, with `evict_after_ms` set, no record it may give up; the refusal changes no record. At the capacity, when no sweep that was due found an expired record, the new conversation takes its room from the records closed by both sides: every one of them goes, whatever `evict_after_ms` is, and no other record (`flow::theorem_g6q_the_closed_yield`, `flow::theorem_g6q_no_refusal_over_a_closed_record`); that pass runs only while a mark is set, which a segment that closes a record for both sides sets and the pass clears, so a pass is paid for by a finished conversation (`flow::theorem_g6r_a_clear_mark_holds_none`, `flow::theorem_g6r_a_set_mark_is_a_closed_conversation`, and the pass's own requirement). With `evict_after_ms` set and no such record, a due sweep that found nothing expired gives up one record that has not expired and the new conversation takes its room: the first by class (closed by both sides; closed by one; never answered by the box; answered, open, and idle for `evict_after_ms`), then by deadline, then by its place in the table; every other record is unchanged, and a record the box has answered, that no side has closed, is never given up within `evict_after_ms` of its last frame (`flow::theorem_g6n_floor`, `flow::theorem_g6n_answered_is_kept`, `flow::theorem_g6n_one`); with it unset no record that has not expired is given up but one closed by both sides (`flow::theorem_g6n_off`). The table sweeps every expired record, and no unexpired one, only for a frame that would open a record it does not hold, at its capacity, at most once in its interval when the interval is positive and time does not run backwards (SW1, NC1, NC26); the instant of the last sweep moves then and at no other time, and `clear` forgets it. Ending a port removes every record of that protocol and box port, of every client, and no other | [`flow::Flows::reply`], [`flow::Flows::inbound`], [`flow::Flows::end_port`], `flow::finv`, `flow::inbound_post`, `flow::inbound_stamp`, `flow::inbound_mark`, `flow::reply_mark` | Verus; Kani for every step of a table with no `evict_after_ms`, the pass and the mark included, per shape, behind `kani-full`, and for the choice of the record given up (`Flows::victim`, per shape); the step that gives a record up is checked by Verus alone |
//! | G6c | The reply table says how many records it can hold and whether it is at that number; the number is fixed when the table is made and no operation changes it (`flow::same_frame`), and a frame is refused for room only when the table is at it (G6) | [`flow::Flows::capacity`], [`flow::Flows::is_full`] | Verus |
//! | G5  | A DNS answer is matched only to the box's own unexpired question with the same name, transaction id and port, and consumes it; an address enters the admitted table only through that match, under the matched name, with a window from the instant of the match; an address has one entry; another name's entry, expired or not, is only extended by a later answer and stays under that name; the answering name's own expired addresses are released first, and one of them is held afterwards only as a fresh entry of this answer; after a match that carries addresses, at any instant but the last a `u64` can name, the name holds no expired address, and an address is refused only when no name holds it and the name is at its quota or the table at its capacity; a frame to an undeclared destination is refused exactly when it has no live flow record and its destination no live admission; a flow record is opened only inside a live window and never for a closing segment; a question or a flow is refused for room only when its row, or its table, is full of entries that have not expired, and making room removes one expired entry, of its own row when the row is at its quota, and nothing when there is room | `pins::Pins::resolve`, [`pins::Pins::ask`], [`pins::Pins::egress`], `pins::pinv`; of one answer, from `resolve`'s guarantee alone: `pins::theorem_g5_new_is_the_names`, `pins::theorem_g5_first_owner`, `pins::theorem_g5_released_quota_is_live`, `pins::theorem_gd_a_admitted_only_by_its_question`, `pins::theorem_gd_b_first_owner_keeps`, `pins::theorem_gd_c_room_admits_all`, `pins::theorem_gd_d_entered_in_order`, `pins::theorem_gd_e_released_first`, `pins::theorem_gd_f_expired_comes_back_fresh`; of room: `pins::theorem_g5_ask_refuses_only_for_room`, `pins::theorem_g5_admit_refuses_only_for_room` | Verus; Kani, with the per-shape harnesses behind `kani-full` |
//! | V1  | Every frame's source is put in exactly one class (no source, cohort, node, a registered row, an unregistered lease, unknown) by its address, family and mark, the plan, the rows, whether the node's baseline is in force and the cohort's spelling alone; `Row(i)` is the first row holding the address; at most one row holds an address, and every row's address is one the plan could lease or the node's | [`origin::Sources::origin`], `origin::theorem_rows_are_leases` | Verus |
//! | V2  | `row::decide_row` returns the decision table `row::row_spec`: the first arm whose condition holds | [`row::decide_row`] | Verus |
//! | V3 to V8 | Of that table: link-local and loopback are never admitted; the gateway only at TCP or UDP port 53; the proxy only on the lane; the alias only when open; a private address other than the alias only when the row's allowed subnets cover it; a denied subnet only by an arm that reads no subnet list (ARP, the lane, the resolver) | `row::theorem_v3` to `row::theorem_v8` | Verus |
//! | N1  | A reset a box sends is admitted as a refusal exactly when it names this box, is RST with ACK, sequence 0 and no payload, and the table holds an unexpired note for the same client and ports whose number is the reset's acknowledgement; so it answers the latest connect noted on those ports and no earlier one, no operation but a connect on those ports putting a number there; it consumes that note and no other; only a SYN without ACK or RST addressed to this box is noted, and there is one note per client and pair of ports; a connect is refused a note only when its client's row, at its quota, or the table is full of notes that have not expired | [`notes::Notes::refusal`], [`notes::Notes::note_connect`], `notes::theorem_n1_latest_connect_only`, `notes::theorem_n1_connect_elsewhere_adds_nothing`, `notes::theorem_n1_refusal_adds_nothing` | Verus; Kani, with the per-shape harnesses behind `kani-full` |
//! | J1  | A frame from a registered row is decided by the first of: a reply on a live inbound flow, the refusal of a noted connect, the stateless arms, then, for an undeclared subnet only, a DNS admission, the alias by name, a box name, the box-name rule being read only when it was computed for this frame's destination (`judge::theorem_j1_box_name_is_for_this_destination`; the gate's and the split entry's rule is this frame's: `gate::theorem_bn1_the_rule_is_this_frames`, `split::theorem_sp1_the_rule_is_this_frames`); each result changes at most one table besides the reply table, from which a lookup that does not admit removes the frame's own expired record; nothing toward the proxy is admitted as a reply or a refusal; no table admits a frame for a box other than its own | [`judge::judge_row`] | Verus |
//! | NV1 to NV7, NJ1 | The node's decision equals its table; of that table: the proxy is never admitted, link-local and loopback never, the gateway only at port 53, the alias only when open, a private address other than the alias only under the baseline, and otherwise only an address the caller says a registered row holds (`dst_is_row`, which `Gate::egress` computes from its rows, GE1) other than the node's own, the resolver or a baseline subnet; a DNS admission lifts only TCP to port 443 through DNS tables whose owner is the frame's source; `Gate::egress` hands it the node's own (GE1) | [`node::decide_node`], [`node::judge_node`] | Verus |
//! | CV1 to CV8, CJ1 | The cohort's decision equals its table; of that table: an ARP packet is admitted, as a row's and the node's are; with no member no IPv4 frame is admitted; the gateway only at port 53; the proxy only on a lane some member declares; the alias only when open; link-local and loopback never, but for the proxy's address, which for a plan inside 100.64.0.0/10 is in neither (`cohort::theorem_cv6_shared`); any other admit has a member whose own rules, decided as a row's, admit the frame (`cohort::theorem_cv7_member_is_its_row`) and for whom it is no infrastructure destination; a DNS admission is asked for only when a name-declaring member refused an undeclared subnet, and lifts only through DNS tables whose owner is the frame's source; `Gate::egress` hands it the cohort's own (GE1) | [`cohort::decide_cohort`], [`cohort::judge_cohort`] | Verus |
//! | J2, NJ2, CJ2 | A frame that reaches a decision without ports (a fragment that is not the first, or a protocol that has none; RT2) passes no opening that names a port, from a row, the node or the cohort: it is never a reply on an inbound flow or the refusal of a noted connect, never on the proxy lane, never to the resolver; to the gateway and to the proxy's address it is refused; to the host alias it is refused unless the alias is open, which is never said of a frame without ports (AO2); the node's DNS admission, which is for port 443, never lifts it; a row's or the cohort's DNS admission admits it by the destination's window alone and records no flow; the reply table and the notes are unchanged. It is still admitted where the address and protocol alone decide: rules, a baseline subnet, a registered row's address for the node, a DNS admission's window, a box name | `judge::theorem_j2_no_port_opening_admits_a_frame_without_ports`, `node::theorem_nj2_no_port_opening_admits_a_frame_without_ports`, `cohort::theorem_cj2_no_port_opening_admits_a_frame_without_ports` | Verus |
//! | NJ3 | A DNS admission lifts a frame of the node only when the stateless arm is `Unpinned` and the frame is TCP to port 443; `is_fetch_shaped` says whether a frame has that shape, so a caller that holds the node's tables itself holds no port number | [`node::is_fetch_shaped`], `node::theorem_nj3_the_node_lift_is_for_fetches` | Verus |
//! | RT1 | Every frame a guest sends is routed to exactly one decision by its source address, family and mark, the plan, the rows, whether the node's baseline is in force and the cohort's spelling: the cohort's, the node's, a registered row's, or one of three refusals (the control surface, an unregistered lease, an unknown address); a refused frame's refusal is the control surface when it is a non-DNS frame to the gateway; nothing from an unregistered or unknown source reaches a decision that can admit | [`route::route`] | Verus |
//! | RT2 | For every byte string and the facts read from it: a frame that reaches routing with ports took them from a whole TCP or UDP header on a first fragment; a TCP or UDP frame that reaches routing without ports is a fragment that is not the first; TCP or UDP on a first fragment without a whole header is never a frame: it is refused before routing, whatever its source, and no decision or table sees it | [`route::frame_of`], `route::theorem_rt2_ports_come_from_a_whole_header` | Verus |
//! | GE1 | `Gate::egress` decides a guest frame in one call: it routes by source, hands the decision that source's own rules and tables and no other's, and changes nothing else; each result carries the routed decision's own guarantee; the tables it reads belong to the frame's source address; a frame from no row, not the node and not the cohort is refused and `Decision::admits` is false of it | [`gate::Gate::egress`] | Verus |
//! | GI1 | `Gate::ingress` records a frame toward a guest in the node's tables when it is addressed to the node while the baseline is in force, in the cohort's when it is addressed to the cohort's own address under that spelling, also while the baseline is in force, and otherwise in those of the first row holding the destination, with IG1 about those tables and "published" read from the gate's own ledger; no table turns the frame away as another box's; a frame to no row is delivered and nothing recorded; nothing else changes | [`gate::Gate::ingress`] | Verus |
//! | GR1 | `Gate::register` adds one row, after the others, exactly when the tables belong to the address, no row holds it and a row may hold it (a lease, or the node's), and otherwise changes nothing; `Gate::retire` removes one; neither changes any other row, the node, the cohort, the ledger or the alias; a registered row's tables belong to its address and hold nothing, whatever they held before; tables that hold nothing admit nothing, so no record of an address's earlier holder in the row's own tables admits a frame from its next; other boxes' tables, and the node's and the cohort's, are not emptied | [`gate::Gate::register`], [`gate::Gate::retire`], `gate::theorem_fresh_admits_nothing` | Verus |
//! | GC1 | `Gate::clear_at` and `Gate::clear_node` empty the named tables of one row or of the node and change nothing else: the row, its rules and name, the tables' owner and settings all stay; a cleared reply table is as one that has not swept, and a cleared DNS table's epoch is raised, until it is the largest a `u64` holds | [`gate::Gate::clear_at`], [`gate::Gate::clear_node`] | Verus |
//! | GD0 | A gate starts with every DNS table empty, whatever the tables it was built from held; `Gate::dns_ask` records a question in the table the query's source and mark name, as `Pins::ask` promises, and changes nothing else, and a query from no row, not the node and not the cohort is recorded nowhere; `Gate::dns_expire` removes expired questions of one table and nothing else | [`gate::Gate::new`], [`gate::Gate::dns_ask`], [`gate::Gate::dns_expire`], `gate::theorem_gd0_a_new_gate_lifts_nothing` | Verus |
//! | GD1 | A DNS reply addressed to `dst` is offered to the table an unmarked query from `dst` would be recorded in and, under the mark spelling at the node's address, to the cohort's table as well; each table offered a name and addresses changes as `Pins::resolve` promises of them, and a table offered no name, or not offered, is as it was; nothing else changes (no reply record, no note, no other row, no ledger entry); so an address is admitted in a table only if that table held a live question for the name it was given | [`gate::Gate::dns_answer`], `gate::theorem_gd1_a_answer_reaches_the_asked_table`, `gate::theorem_gd1_a_offered_tables_differ`, `gate::theorem_gd1_b_unnamed_table_is_untouched`, `gate::theorem_gd1_d_admitted_by_its_own_question` | Verus |
//! | GB1 | `Gate::adopt_baseline` sets the baseline's phase and entries and empties the node's DNS tables, in the node's own tables and in the row at the node's address if there is one, and leaves the cohort's DNS tables as they were; `Gate::clear_cohort_dns` empties the cohort's DNS tables and nothing else; neither changes a reply record, a note, another row, the ledger or the alias state | [`gate::Gate::adopt_baseline`], [`gate::Gate::clear_cohort_dns`], `gate::theorem_gb1_a_adopting_empties_the_nodes_dns`, `gate::theorem_gb1_b_adopting_keeps_the_cohorts_dns` | Verus |
//! | BN1 | The box-name rule equals its table: silent unless the source row lists box names and the destination of an IPv4 frame is another registered row that is not the node; refused for a task row; otherwise allowed exactly when the source lists the target's name | `gate::box_name_spec` | Verus |
//! | IG1 | A frame toward a box is dropped only as a proxy opening at a published port or as a new flow the reply table refuses at its capacity, or as a frame for another box handed to these tables, which `Gate::ingress` never does; a dropped frame changes no table's contents, and the refused flow moves the reply table's instant of the last sweep exactly when a sweep was due; the proxy's address never opens a record and is never noted; a published port touches only the reply table and an unpublished one only the notes, and only TCP is noted | [`ingress::observe`] | Verus |
//! | IG2 | For every byte string and the facts read from it: a frame toward a guest reaches a box's tables only as a TCP or UDP first fragment with a whole header; such a fragment without a whole header is dropped and nothing is recorded; a later fragment, a protocol without ports, an ARP packet, IPv6, a VLAN tag and another EtherType are delivered and nothing is recorded; bytes too short for their header, an ARP packet of another shape and an IPv4 header with a bad version, header length or total length are dropped and nothing is recorded, under a rule of their own (`ingress-truncated-ipv4`): for every reason the reader gives, dropped toward a guest exactly when reported leaving one as no frame (IG3) | [`ingress::received_of`], `ingress::theorem_ig2_the_tables_see_whole_headers`, `ingress::theorem_ig3_no_frame_both_ways` | Verus |
//! | L1  | A listener has at most one holder; a publish for a held listener changes nothing; a row's publish past the bound is refused and evicts nothing, an interim one evicts the oldest interim forward and no other; a retraction removes its listener's forward and no other; `dials` is true exactly when a held forward dials that address and port | [`ledger::Ledger::publish`], [`ledger::Ledger::retract`], [`ledger::Ledger::dials`] | Verus |
//! | AO1 to AO5 | The host alias is open exactly as its specification says; never to a frame with no port, never to a box that does not name the host, never at a port one of the product's own listeners holds (the node's baseline fetch over TCP excepted), and otherwise only at a configured exposure's own protocol and port | [`alias::alias_open`] | Verus |
//! | GP1 | A retraction ends the reply records at its forward's address, protocol and inside port exactly when no forward left dials them: afterwards no reply table of that address holds such a record, for any client; while another forward dials them nothing but the ledger changes; no table of another address, no note and no DNS table is ever touched | [`gate::Gate::retract`], `gate::theorem_gp1_a_retracted_port_holds_no_record`, `gate::theorem_gp1_b_standing_forward_keeps_the_records`, `gate::theorem_gp1_c_other_tables_are_untouched` | Verus |
//! | GD2 | `Gate::dns_answer` drops every address that fails the floor before any table sees it: an entry a DNS table did not hold before a reply has an address that is not in 100.64.0.0/10, not in the plan's block (no box's lease, nor the gateway, the node, the proxy, the cohort's address or the host alias) and not in a range no name may name, whatever the caller filtered; a list that already passes is not changed; a list of more than 65,535 addresses, which no DNS message can carry, is dropped whole and counted as dropped | [`gate::Gate::dns_answer`], `gate::theorem_gd2_a_admitted_addresses_pass_the_floor`, `gate::theorem_gd2_b_the_floor_excludes_the_block`, `rebind::theorem_floored_identity`, `rebind::theorem_floor_is_below_the_check` | Verus |
//! | TA1 to TA5 | One entry takes a DNS answer's addresses as the reply carried them: with no live question for the name nothing changes; otherwise each address is put to the rebinding check (RB1), only those that pass reach the box's tables, which change as `Pins::resolve` promises of exactly those, and the result says of each address, in order, that the check refused it and why, or that it is held afterwards, under which name and until when, or that no name holds it, which happens only with the name at its quota or the table at its capacity; an admission the table holds afterwards and did not hold before is the answer's name's and its address passed the check | [`answer::answer`], `answer::answer_post`, `answer::theorem_ta4_no_room_is_quota_or_capacity`, `answer::theorem_ta5_a_new_admission_passed_the_check` | Verus |
//! | GM1 | `Gate::set_members` replaces the cohort's members and changes nothing else; `Gate::tables_at` and the tables' `has_live`, `asked_live` and `admitted_live` read a table and change nothing, each returning what its specification says is held and unexpired; each table reader requires its module's invariant, which the gate's invariant supplies | [`gate::Gate::set_members`], [`gate::Gate::tables_at`], [`flow::Flows::has_live`], [`notes::Notes::has_live`], [`pins::Pins::asked_live`], [`pins::Pins::admitted_live`] | Verus |
//! | SB1 | A table kept as a slab and a sorted array of positions keeps G7 as `Pool` does, with the same guarantees for lookup, replacement, insertion and `insert_reclaiming`, except that it refuses a capacity above 32,767 (`slab::MAX_CAP`) and six of its readers (`get`, `entry`, `row_count`, `live_count`, `any_live`, `holder`) require the table's invariant where `Pool`'s do not; its search reads at most `log2(n) + 1` entries of a table of `n` and at least `log2(n + 1)`, so at most 13 for 4,096 and at most 15 for any table the constructor allows; finding the place of a row and key reads at most `log2(n) + 2` entries, and at least `log2(n + 1) + 1` when the place is inside the table. The count is a result only the verifier sees, raised by hand beside each read; the public functions do not carry it | [`slab::Slab::insert`], [`slab::Slab::entry`]; the count: the guarantees of `slab::Slab::lower` and `slab::Slab::place`, with `slab::theorem_sb1_probes_in_numbers` for the numbers | Verus; Kani per shape: every slab length and fill of the capacities 0 to 3 (one harness for each), the search and the readers in the default set, eight mutators in seven harness pairs under `kani-full` (`release_expired` and `remove_ends` share one); none for `set`, `drop_expired` or `clear` |
//! | SP1 | A frame can be decided without the whole gate: `Config::egress_for` and `Config::ingress_for` read the configuration, write nothing, and name the route, the lifts, the alias state and the row that GE1 and GI1 say the gate decides with; that the judge they name, run on that box's tables, is the judge the gate runs is shown by example, not stated, and for a frame toward a guest needs `published` read from the ledger, which the configuration does not hold; a configuration without one declaration for each registered row routes nothing | [`split::Config::egress_for`], [`split::Config::ingress_for`], `split::theorem_sp1_egress_reads_the_gates`, `split::theorem_sp1_ingress_reads_the_gates` | Verus; and by example in two of the gate's unit checks, `plan_then_judge_decides_as_the_whole_gate_does` and `a_configuration_short_of_a_declaration_routes_nothing` |
//! | EP1, EP2, EP3 | One call for the part of a decision that reads no table, from the frame's bytes: `egress_first` returns what the reader, the conversion (which refuses a short transport header), the route and the routed source's decision table give, in that order, and `ingress_first` what the reader, the conversion and the choice of tables give; a caller reads no byte of the frame to decide. Whatever the judge then returns, a stateless result is the routed source's table arm (the arm given, unless that was the DNS refusal below, which the judges do not know and a caller acts on as a drop without asking one), and the judge admits only as a reply, as a refusal, by an arm that admits, or by a lift of the one arm a lift may turn (an undeclared subnet for a row, `Unpinned` for the node, `NeedsPin` for the cohort). EP3: a row that declares no subnet and no DNS host is given the resolver's arm only for a whole DNS query that asks only about names in the zone (DQ2), and so is a cohort none of whose members declares either; any other UDP datagram it sends the resolver at port 53 is refused under the rule `egress-deny-all-dns-query`; that the resolver hands no zone-only query on is registry row NC39. What is no frame is reported under the rule of the reader's reason | [`split::Config::egress_first`], [`split::Config::ingress_first`], [`report::arm`], `split::theorem_ep2_a_row_is_admitted_by_its_arm_or_a_named_way`, `split::theorem_ep2_the_node_is_admitted_by_its_arm_or_a_named_way`, `split::theorem_ep2_the_cohort_is_admitted_by_its_arm_or_a_named_way`, `split::theorem_ep3_a_row_that_names_nothing_asks_only_for_zone_names`, `split::theorem_ep3_a_cohort_that_names_nothing_asks_only_for_zone_names`, [`report::not_a_frame`] | Verus |
//! | CF1 | The configuration a frame's decision reads changes only through its own operations, each keeping it sound (one declaration a registered row, in row order) and changing what its contract names and nothing else: a row is added after every row there is; a row is removed with its declaration, later rows moving down by one; a row's declaration is replaced in place, no index moving; one name changes; the members, the alias state and the baseline are each replaced whole | [`split::Config::register`], [`split::Config::retire_at`], [`split::Config::restate_at`], [`split::Config::rename_at`], [`split::Config::set_members`], [`split::Config::set_alias`], [`split::Config::adopt_baseline`] | Verus |
//! | RB1 | The check on a DNS answer's address equals its table: never a denied subnet, never link-local, loopback, this-host, multicast or reserved space, never one of the switch's own listed addresses; for a name outside the box zone never an address in 100.64.0.0/10, so, for a plan inside that range, none of the plan's block; for a box-zone name only an address in that range; a private address only when the allowed subnets cover it | [`rebind::rebind`], `rebind::theorem_rb1_refused_ranges`, `rebind::theorem_rb1_ordinary_name_reaches_no_box`, `rebind::theorem_rb1_zone_name_stays_in_the_plane` | Verus, Kani |
